diff --git a/launcher/30.40-ANALYSIS.md b/launcher/30.40-ANALYSIS.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..12301cb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/launcher/30.40-ANALYSIS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,86 @@
+# Supplied 30.40 executable and startup investigation
+
+Inspected 2026-09-17. The upload was described as Fortnite 30.40; its protected
+contents do not expose a release string that independently verifies that label.
+
+## File examined
+
+- Filename: `FortniteClient-Win64-Shipping.exe`
+- Size: 320,059,920 bytes
+- SHA256: `81302ab997ce7a4015ded4cd60cbbf94a4ecd5810c4b8a1c8a2147aec3053c98`
+- Format: x64 PE32+, three unnamed sections, image base `0x140000000`.
+- Entry-point RVA: zero. Import directory: absent. TLS callback RVA: `0x1312ec00`.
+- The large payload section is opaque; a sampled 1 MiB has approximately
+  8 bits of entropy per byte. No ASCII/UTF-16 Fortnite release marker was found.
+- The ZIP contains only the executable, with no game assets or runtime dump.
+
+The executable was inspected as data. It was not run, modified, decrypted or
+published with this source. Regression tests use a small synthetic PE fixture.
+The compiled C# preflight detector was also run against the actual uploaded file
+and recognized this stub. All 21 new startup checks passed locally before the
+Windows package build.
+
+## Why Emilyfn reports a module timeout
+
+The diagnostic ZIP contains three new host attempts that stop at **Loading local
+authentication**, exactly 20 seconds after process creation. None of their process
+IDs appears in the authentication startup log. That log is left over from an older
+24.40 attempt; the server/client logs record successful SDK initialization only
+for earlier 27.11 and 24.40 sessions. No new login or server-module initialization
+is recorded for the failing attempts.
+
+Static disassembly of the supplied executable's TLS callback shows it constructs
+the message **"Please launch the game through the Epic Games launcher."** using
+stack byte writes. It resolves `LoadLibraryA`, loads `user32.dll`, resolves and
+calls `MessageBoxA`, then calls `ZwTerminateProcess` after the dialog returns.
+There is no normal direct-launch entry point in this file.
+
+This is consistent with the new process being blocked inside its own startup
+dialog before Emilyfn's authentication loader thread can run. Windows serializes
+new thread startup with process/DLL initialization; see Microsoft's
+[CreateRemoteThread documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/processthreadsapi/nf-processthreadsapi-createremotethread).
+We did not capture a live thread stack, so the user's exact waiting instruction
+has not been observed. The incompatible startup stub itself is confirmed from
+the supplied file. Increasing the timeout does not provide the missing startup
+preparation or a compatible game image.
+
+Emilyfn 0.3.1 checks for this specific combination of PE headers, executable TLS
+callback and complete startup-message instructions before import, installation
+or launch. It does not reject a file merely for using TLS or having missing version
+metadata. A saved manual release label cannot override the check. Other loader
+timeouts now name the DLL, process ID and wait duration. Game DLLs are unchanged.
+
+This is an executable compatibility/error-reporting fix, not a claim that Fortnite
+30.40 is playable. A compatible installation of the same exact release is still
+required before native SDK and match behavior can be tested.
+
+## LEGO Fortnite and Fortnite Festival
+
+**Neither mode is implemented or validated in the current Emilyfn game server.**
+
+The supplied executable has no readable ASCII or UTF-16 matches for `LEGO`,
+`LegoFortnite`, `FortniteFestival`, `Festival`, `Juno`, `JunoGame`, or `Sparks`.
+The same scan also finds no `AthenaGameMode`, `Helios_Terrain`, or release marker.
+Because the main payload is opaque, these negative results do **not** prove
+that the underlying Fortnite build lacks either mode. They provide no usable
+class layouts, function addresses, playlists or asset paths for implementing them.
+
+The current source establishes the following separate limitations:
+
+| Area | Current implementation | Work still required |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Chapter 5 startup | `Reboot2711/RuntimeProfile.h` selects `Helios_Terrain` | Exact mode-specific startup and content loading |
+| Playlist | `Erbium/Erbium/Public/Configuration.h` selects Battle Royale Solo | Verified playlists and compatible game-mode classes |
+| Game state and players | `Erbium/FortniteGame/Private/FortGameMode.cpp` expects Athena state, controllers and pawns | Mode-specific spawning, state and replication |
+| LEGO worlds | No LEGO-specific implementation found in server/client/SDK source | World creation, loading, persistence, inventory and join behavior |
+| Festival sessions | No Festival-specific implementation found in server/client/SDK source | Stage/session state, song content, timing and player behavior |
+
+The older Athena concert references in the source are not a Fortnite Festival
+implementation. Accepting a release number or adding a mode button would not
+implement these systems.
+
+The next useful inputs are a readable/unpacked executable or matching SDK/reflection
+dump from the exact 30.40 build, plus its relevant asset inventory. Asset names
+alone are not sufficient to run the modes; the corresponding content and backend
+contracts must then be implemented and tested on Windows. This update does not
+add a guessed mode selector or claim that either mode works.
